# Southmeadow (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): grain fields and orchards in the far south. Cheerful; harvest,
# fruit, chaff and windfalls. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar southmeadow
greet = Good harvest | Harvest to you
greet.broad = Good 'arvest
farewell = Ripe days | Good harvest
agree = Ripe and right | Aye, ripe
refuse = Not this season
surprise = Well, pick me | Bless the barley
annoyed = Blight take it | Wormy luck
very = full
good = ripe
bad = blighted | wormy
luck = a windfall
child = sprout
pet!! = my apple | my plum
hot = sun-ripe | baking
tired = harvested
nonsense = chaff
tag = , eh
oath! = Sheaf and sickle | Blight take it | Apples and ale
home = my own orchard
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = friend | neighbour
address[other.stage=young] = sprout | young 'un
bed = my straw | my bed
cold = chilly | nippy
dear! = my plum | my sweet | love
drunk = cider-merry | ripe
hungry = starved | hollow
hurt = bruised | sore
little = a pip | a little
money = coin | penny-pieces
rain = shower | rain
robbery = Robbery, ripe as you like | Picked clean, like a windfall
saying! = What's ripe is ripe | There's always another harvest | The sun comes round again
thanks = Thank you kindly | Bless you
work = the harvest | the picking | the work
worthless = chaff | wormy
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = pay | harvest money
boss = the farmer | the master
busy = harvest-busy | run off my paws
slow = slow | lazy as July
cheated = robbed of the sheaf | done
mum! = Mam | Mother | Ma
dad! = Da | Pa | Father
gran = Granny | Gran
grandad = Grandad | Gaffer
supper = supper | harvest supper
toffs = the city quality | the fine folk
skint = skint | not a pip
posh = grand | fancy
busybody = chatterer | gossip
fool = you turnip | you great windfall
daft = daft as a scarecrow | daft
toast = Good harvest | Here's to the apple | Ripe days
drink = a cup of cider | a jar
wind = harvest wind | a breeze
ale = cider | ale
thief = orchard-robber | thief
grave = the green ground | the churchyard
grub = victuals | grub
tasty = ripe | lovely
snug = snug | sun-warm
beasts = the stock | the beasts
poorly = wilted | poorly
remedy = honey and apple vinegar | a nettle brew | camomile
olddays = the old harvests | days gone by
blessing = Good harvest to you | Ripe days | The sun keep you
bless = Sun keep you | Good harvest
someday = some fine harvest | one day
far = a long walk north | a fair step
stranger = northerner | stranger
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = Fresh from the fields. Have a look. | What'll it be, neighbour? | Ripe and ready.
shop_dear = A thin harvest makes dear bread. | Dear till the next reaping. | The fields set the price.
shop_sold = Ripe days to you. | There, and fresh. | Good harvest, that.
shop_bye = Ripe days. | Good harvest to you. | Come back at the reaping.
shop_bare = Gleaned to the stubble. | Bare as a winter furrow. | Nothing left till harvest.
talk_open = What's on your mind, neighbour? | Out with it, then. | Something you're after?
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There's work, neighbour, if you'll take it. | I could use another pair of paws. | Something wants doing before the reaping.
work_taken = Good. Don't let it spoil. | Good. Get it in before the rain. | Settled, then.
work_paid = Here, as promised, neighbour. | Paid, and a good harvest to you. | Fairly done. There you are.
work_back = No harm done. Another will reap it. | Fair enough. It'll keep a season. | Better now than left to rot.
work_none = Nothing going, neighbour. | No work till the reaping. | Nothing needs doing just now.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Come in from the fields. Bed, board or a drink? | A bed or a bite, neighbour? | What'll it be?
inn_bed = Upstairs, clean straw and all. There's a chest for your things. | Your bed's upstairs. Ripe days.
watch_greet = The watch, neighbour. Out with it. | Keep the peace and we'll all get the harvest in.
church_greet = Good harvest to you. Come in. | The sun keep you. All are welcome here.
learn_offer = It's a trade that feeds a family. | Long days, but good ones.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... shut till cockcrow. | Zzz... come back at sunup, neighbour.
news_none = Quiet as a fallow field. | Nothing new since the sowing. | Not that I've heard, neighbour.
gossip_none = Chaff and chatter. Not for me. | Nothing I'd repeat, neighbour.
trouble_none = Nothing a good season won't mend. | Nothing, neighbour.
trouble_thanks = Good harvest to you. I won't forget it. | Bless you, neighbour. Thank you.
way_ask = Where are you headed, neighbour? | Lost in the lanes?
job_busy = Whatever the season wants. | This and that, neighbour.
work_declined = Suit yourself, neighbour. | Another season, then.
inn_rest = Sit by the fire, neighbour. A bed upstairs rests you properly. | Take the bench. Rest your legs.
watch_noted = Noted. We'll weed them out. | Good. The watch thanks you, neighbour.
watch_bye = Keep the peace, neighbour. | Move along, then.
learn_taken = Good. Be here at first light, and stay near me. | Then you're mine to teach, neighbour. Stay close.
receiver_ask = Something for me, neighbour? | For me? Well, pick me.
inn_helper = I only help here, neighbour. Ask #here.innkeeper# for a bed. | #here.innkeeper# keeps the house. I carry the cups.

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): Southmeadow's hedge, account, preclose, excuse and the rest (plain.grammar
# says what each is). Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
hedge = Ah, bless me | Bless me, but | Now then, neighbour
account = I've my own row to hoe | There's too much ripening at once
ack = Well, well, bless me | Fancy that, now
pivot = Which reminds me, bless me. | That's the same field, near enough.
anyway = Anyway, enough chaff. | Still, let that one fall.
later = after the picking | come harvest
resume = Back to the field, then | Where was I, bless me
preclose = Well. I'm no scarecrow | Still. Early start tomorrow
excuse = The picking won't wait | Early start tomorrow
knew = Old chaff, that | Heard it in the fields
sympathy = That's a blighted lot | A sour crop, that
concede = Fair as fruit | Right, maybe, bless you
insist = Fence-post firm, I am | Mark my words, bless me
warn = Mind the nettles | Watch the ditch
welcome_back = Back for a second picking | You again, bless you
welcome = Come on in, bless you | Always room in the field
sorry = No offence, bless you | That came out wormy
forgive = No harm, bless you | Forgotten like last year's chaff
deflect = No bother, bless you | Any time, bless you
no_matter = Another harvest, then | No matter, bless you
go_ahead = Ask away, bless you | Spill it like apples
put_off = Not while there's picking | After the harvest, maybe
vent = Between us and the hedgerow | Truth be told, my plum
reassure = It'll come good, bless you | Every blight passes
congrats = A fine harvest for you | Ripe for you, that
demur = That's chaff to me | I'd not pick that
doubt = Sounds wormy | Not till it's ripe
listen = Listen, bless you | Here

# Phase 8 (doc 84 §33): the shared lines' words, as Southmeadow says them. Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
marvel = Well, bless the apples | Pick me, now | Bless me, that's new fruit
pity = Blight on a good tree, that | That's a sour harvest
gladnews = That's ripe news | Fruit on the bough, that
uneasy = That's blight on the wind | I don't like the smell of that crop
grumble = Blighted, all of it | Wormy as last year, bless you
notsobad = There's fruit on it yet | I've seen thinner harvests
dunno = That's all the gleaning | Not a pip more, bless you
hearsay = Field-talk, that's all | Heard it over the hedge
howso = Ripe, is it? How so | How's that, bless you
rest = Soft earth to the soul | Rest easy in the orchard
# Greetings and closings for rules/shared8_turns.talk (plain.grammar's s8_* say who; here, the dialect's own).
s8_hello = Harvest to you, neighbour | Ah, bless you | Good harvest, friend | Well, bless me, hello
s8_okay = Off you pick, then | Bless you, go on | The furrows won't wait | Ripe time to go
s8_bye = Ripe days, neighbour | Bless you, then | Mind the furrows | Good gleaning
s8_byeback = And ripe days to you | And bless you | Good gleaning to you | Mind the furrows, now
s8_nevermind = Another harvest, then | Bless you, no matter | Let it lie fallow

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7): plain's short answers ("Good.", "Always.", "It is.") as Southmeadow says them.
# Drafts, to be tuned by ear; one left out falls back to plain's words.
bc_good = Ripe. | Ripe, that.
bc_always = Every season, like the swallows. | Same as every harvest.
bc_thank_you = Thank you kindly. | Bless you, that's lovely.
bc_it_is = It is, ripe and right. | That it is.
bc_ha = Ha! Lovely. | Ha! Ripe, that one.
bc_i_will = I will, gladly. | I will, come harvest.
bc_never = Not in a month of harvests. | Not this season.
bc_probably = Likely as windfalls. | Like as not.
bc_we_will_see = We'll see come harvest. | We'll see what ripens.
bc_maybe = Maybe so. | Maybe, come the season.
bc_me_too = Same field. | Me too, bless me.
bc_it_does = It does, ripe and right. | That it does.
bc_again = Again. Wormy luck. | Again, like weeds.
bc_lovely = Lovely as a plum. | Ripe and lovely.
bc_every_year = Every harvest, sure as sunrise. | Every year, like the apples.
bc_wise = Wise as a farmer. | Wise as an old orchard-keeper.
bc_i_know = Don't I know it. | I know, bless me.
bc_so_they_say = So they say in the fields. | So the farmers say.
bc_again_q = Again? Bless the barley. | What, again?
bc_of_course = Of course, bless you. | Course, sure as harvest.
bc_about_time = About time, bless it. | About time. Lazy as July.
bc_mine_too = Mine too, bless it. | Same field.
bc_ill_try = I'll have a go. | I'll try, bless me.
bc_nor_me = Nor me, bless me.
bc_they_do = They do, bless them. | That they do.
bc_poor_soul = Poor lamb. | Poor soul. Blighted luck.
bc_i_wont = I won't, bless me. | Not a pip of it.
bc_every_one = Every apple of them. | Every last one.
bc_not_me = Not me, bless me.
bc_very_good = Full ripe. | Ripe and right.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then, bless you. | Go on, pick away.
bc_barely = Barely, by a pip.
bc_lucky_you = A windfall for you. | Lucky plum.
bc_every_night = Every night, after the picking. | Every blessed night.
bc_nobody = Not a soul in the fields. | Nobody, bless me.
bc_for_once = For once, bless it. | Once in a blue harvest.
bc_i_hope_so = I hope so, bless it. | Here's hoping for a good crop.
bc_exactly = Ripe and right. | Exactly so.
bc_everything = The whole harvest. | Every last apple.
bc_true = True as the season. | Ripe truth.
bc_mostly = Mostly, bless it. | Mostly, in a good season.
bc_gladly = Gladly, bless you. | Gladly as a harvest.
bc_fair_enough = Fair enough, bless it. | Ripe enough.
bc_sorry = Sorry, bless you.
bc_every_time = Every time, every harvest. | Every picking.
bc_very_bad = Blighted. | Wormy, that.
bc_fair = Fair, bless it.
bc_bless_you = Bless your harvest. | Bless your basket.
bc_always_is = Always is, every season. | Always is, bless it.
bc_right = Right you are. | Right, bless it.
bc_lucky = A windfall. | Lucky as a windfall.
bc_bad_luck = Wormy luck. | Blighted luck.
bc_why_q = Why's that, then? | Why, bless me?
bc_true_enough = Ripe and right. | True enough, bless it.
bc_they_always_do = They always do, every season. | Always do, bless them.
bc_every_day = Every day, sun to sun. | Every blessed day.
bc_very_busy = Harvest-busy. | Run off my paws.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry, bless you. | I'm full sorry.
bc_so_do_i = So do I, bless me. | Same field.
bc_clever = Clever as a crow. | Clever, bless you.
bc_too_late = Too late. The harvest's in. | Past picking.
bc_go_on = Go on, bless you. | Go on, spill it.
bc_we_will = We will, come harvest. | That we will, bless us.
bc_they_are = They are, bless them. | That they are.
bc_so_am_i = So am I, bless me. | Same field.
bc_let_them = Let them, bless them. | Let them. Windfalls for all.
bc_hope_so = Hope so, bless it. | Here's hoping for a good crop.
bc_already = Already, bless it. | Long since.
bc_i_might = I might, come the season. | Might do, bless me.
bc_the_best = Pick of the crop. | The best, ripe as you like.
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to bring it in. | Somebody has to, bless us.
bc_nothing = Not a pip. | Nothing, bless me.
bc_and_q = And then, bless you?
bc_who_q = Who's that, then? | Who, bless me?
bc_it_was = It was, ripe and right. | That it was.
